  "image_name": "33ab51eddbe7cd597a4a378e6948dd9f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how radical-templated synthesis can direct mechanically interlocked molecules toward either rotaxanes or catenanes, and then shows several large examples of these architectures. The top schematic contrasts “stoppering,” which traps a threaded ring on a dumbbell-like axle to give a rotaxane, with “cyclization,” which closes components into interlocked rings to give a catenane under oxidizing conditions.\n\nThe molecular drawings below emphasize positively charged, blue cyclophane-like units assembled around radical sites shown in pink, producing highly interlocked, chain-like or cyclic structures. Some are linear oligomeric rotaxanes with two bulky end groups that prevent unthreading, while others are closed catenanes containing multiple interwoven rings. One comparison highlights a homo[2]catenane versus a mixed or hetero analogue, distinguished by different ring colors.\n\nThe bottom portion illustrates a “cyclic [3]daisy chain” assembly process. A smaller precursor appears to self-assemble through complementary radical interactions into a larger cyclic intermediate, which is then fixed by stoppering and oxidation to yield a robust, highly charged interlocked macrocycle. Overall, the figure conveys that radical recognition is being used as a modular strategy to build increasingly complex mechanically bonded molecules, from simple rotaxanes to large catenanes and daisy chains.",
